  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The District of Columbia has $22B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The District recorded 33,933 awards with an average award size of $649K. Obligations fell down 30.0% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a peak at $36B in FY24, then $31.4B in FY25, and $22B in FY26. The FY21 through FY26 series reads $31B, $31.6B, $33.9B, $36B, $31.4B, $22B. These totals are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations only, so the current-year aggregate is partial for FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has contracting in the District of Columbia changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell down 30.0% year over year. Annual obligations were $31B in FY21, $31.6B in FY22, $33.9B in FY23, $36B in FY24, $31.4B in FY25, and $22B in FY26. The District recorded 33,933 awards in FY2026 to date, with an average award size of $649K. These figures represent contract obligations and the FY2026 total is fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate federal spending in the District of Columbia?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.2B in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Transportation with $3B and the General Services Administration with $2.9B. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry with $3.1B, followed by Engineering Services with $2.7B and Other Computer Related Services with $2.6B. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $2.5B and the Department of State obligated $1.3B. The top vendor by obligations is BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC with $936M."
      }
    ]
  },
